VALUES (
  '2026/05/11/hormuz-ceasefire-fragility-canadian-portfolios',
  'geo', 'article',
  'The Ceasefire Is Holding. The Supply Shock Is Not.',
  'A technical ceasefire between the U.S. and Iran has cooled the worst-case scenario. But the Strait of Hormuz remains largely closed, the IEA calls this the largest supply disruption in market history, and oil prices have a floor the market has not fully priced.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>A formal ceasefire was reached in early April,</strong><span> but it is contested: U.S. and Iranian forces continued exchanging fire through the week of May 3-9, with Iran accusing the U.S. of violations and Washington conducting defensive strikes while insisting the ceasefire remains in effect.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Strait of Hormuz has been effectively closed since late February,</strong><span> disrupting approximately 20% of global seaborne crude oil and significant LNG volumes, in what the IEA has characterized as the largest supply shock in the history of the oil market.</span></li>
<li><strong>Brent crude settled near $101 last week after an exceptionally volatile stretch,</strong><span> having swung between a high of $107 and a low below $89 in a single week as markets reacted to ceasefire signals, fresh clashes, and evolving diplomatic negotiations via Pakistan.</span></li>
<li><strong>JPMorgan has warned that supply buffers are eroding</strong><span> and that even a diplomatic resolution will not immediately restore the estimated one billion barrels of production lost since February, anchoring the oil price floor materially above pre-war levels for a sustained period.</span></li>
<li><strong>Canada''s dual exposure is unlike most G7 nations:</strong><span> as a net oil exporter, Canada benefits from elevated energy prices even as transportation costs, fertiliser prices, and global growth prospects worsen, a split-effect the Bank of Canada is navigating in real time.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>The week of May 3 through 9 produced one of the most volatile stretches in oil markets since the Iran war began in late February. WTI futures swung between $107.46 and $88.66 in a single week before stabilizing near $95 per barrel. Brent settled near $101. The volatility reflected a market trying to price a conflict with no clean resolution timeline: a ceasefire that both parties contest, a Strait of Hormuz that remains largely closed, and diplomatic negotiations through Pakistani intermediaries that have produced no definitive agreement as of May 11.</p>
<p>The underlying supply story has not changed. The IEA estimates the conflict is removing approximately 14 million barrels per day from global supply. QatarEnergy declared force majeure on LNG contracts after Iran struck Qatar''s Ras Laffan facility in March. The UAE and Kuwait have been intercepting hostile drones. The physical market has been operating in crisis mode for ten weeks, and the ceasefire has not materially altered the supply picture.</p>

<h2>The Chain From Hormuz to Canadian Portfolios</h2>
<p>The mechanism connecting the Strait of Hormuz to a Canadian advisor''s client portfolio runs through several distinct channels that cut in different directions. The most direct and positive channel for Canada is energy revenue. Canada is one of the few G7 nations that is a net oil exporter. Canadian oil sands production does not transit Hormuz and is priced relative to WTI, which has risen roughly 56% over the past year. Canadian energy companies have benefited substantially, with Cardinal Energy, Enbridge, and Wheaton Precious Metals all reporting strong Q1 results in the most recent earnings period.</p>
<p>The negative channels are less visible but more pervasive. Jet fuel spiked approximately 95% since the war began, contributing to travel cost inflation. Fertiliser prices have risen because natural gas, used to produce ammonia, has become scarcer through the LNG disruption. The IEA warned in its April Oil Market Report that demand destruction has begun in Asia, projecting a Q2 2026 contraction of roughly 1.5 million barrels per day. That demand destruction threatens the global growth outlook and, through that channel, commodity prices broadly.</p>

