<h2>Ceasefire Credibility and the Pricing Problem</h2>
<p>The repeated pattern in oil markets since late February has been a cycle of diplomatic hope followed by military escalation followed by a repricing that lands somewhere between the prior peak and trough. Brent surged to $119, retreated on ceasefire news toward $88 briefly, and has since settled near $101 as markets balance diplomatic progress against continued physical disruptions. The week of May 3-9 compressed that entire cycle into five trading days.</p>
<p>From a portfolio construction standpoint, this pattern creates a specific risk for Canadian investors with energy overweights. A rapid, credible resolution that restores Hormuz traffic would likely send Brent toward the $75-80 range the Bank of Canada assumed in its baseline forecast: a 20-25% decline from current levels. That scenario arrives alongside the CUSMA review deadline on July 1, compressing two of the TSX''s major risk factors into a single summer window.</p>
<p>JPMorgan''s economists told clients in a May 8 note that supply buffers are eroding and that even after shipping access is restored, roughly one billion barrels of lost production must be replenished before the market normalizes. For Canadian portfolios with energy exposure, that is the base case to plan around: an oil floor meaningfully above pre-war levels even in the resolution scenario, for a period that extends beyond the diplomatic headline.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients have developed headline fatigue from the war. Many have stopped reading closely because the situation shifts daily. Some are quietly pleased their Canadian energy exposure has performed well. Others are anxious about gas prices, food costs, and travel expenses and are connecting that lived experience to investment anxiety. The sense of a world more dangerous than it was in January is shaping conversations even with clients who don''t follow markets closely.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The ceasefire in Iran is real, but the Strait of Hormuz is still largely closed. The reason oil prices have not collapsed is that the physical supply problem has not been solved. JPMorgan put out a note this week saying that even after a diplomatic agreement, the supply that has been lost since February will take time to recover, and the oil price floor will stay elevated for a period. What that means for your portfolio is nuanced. Canadian energy names have benefited, which is a positive if you hold the TSX broadly. But there is a scenario this summer where a credible diplomatic breakthrough sends oil back toward $75-80, and that would hit those same energy names. We are planning for that possibility while recognizing that a quick resolution is not guaranteed. The July CUSMA deadline adds another layer. This is a quarter where scenario planning in your portfolio review genuinely matters.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Clients with concentrated TSX or Canadian energy exposure who face an asymmetric oil-price scenario into the summer; clients with travel plans or businesses with elevated fuel costs.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Balanced portfolio clients where energy gains are being partially offset by transportation and input cost inflation affecting non-energy holdings.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> Clients with global diversified equity exposure, particularly those with U.S. large-cap holdings that have continued to benefit from strong earnings momentum despite the geopolitical backdrop.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify clients with energy sector concentrations above 25% and assess whether their risk tolerance aligns with a scenario where oil returns to $75-80 on diplomatic resolution.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review client portfolios for indirect inflation exposure: businesses with high fuel costs, holdings in airlines, transportation, or fertiliser-dependent agriculture.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Note the July 1 CUSMA deadline and flag clients with manufacturing, automotive, or export-sector exposure for a pre-summer review.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document geopolitical risk conversations per your firm''s compliance requirements.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The Iran ceasefire and what it means for your Canadian portfolio this summer<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
The ceasefire between the U.S. and Iran is holding technically, but the Strait of Hormuz remains largely closed. Oil had its most volatile week of the year last week, swinging nearly $20 in five days before settling near $101. The IEA has called this the largest oil supply disruption in market history.<br><br>
For Canadian investors, that creates specific risks and opportunities heading into summer. I want to walk through two scenarios with you: what your portfolio looks like if oil prices stay elevated, and what it looks like if a credible diplomatic resolution sends prices back toward pre-war levels. Both are plausible and the planning conversation is worth having now. I''ll be in touch to schedule time.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Business owners with fuel or input cost exposure:</strong> The war has structurally increased their operating costs, and many have no financial advisor helping them think about offsetting that through portfolio construction or corporate structure.</p>
<p><strong>DIY investors who bought energy names in February or March:</strong> They made a correct call but now have no framework for managing the exit risk if diplomatic resolution compresses the premium.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I''m reaching out because the Iran ceasefire story is more complicated than it looks, and there are specific implications for Canadian investors that aren''t getting much attention. Oil had its most volatile week of the year last week, and I wanted to share what we''re seeing and whether it''s relevant to your situation.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>A ceasefire that does not reopen Hormuz is a pause, not a resolution. The scenario planning required to manage a Canadian portfolio through that pause, while also watching the CUSMA July deadline, requires a structured analytical framework that a DIY investor tracking headlines simply does not have.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>How are you thinking about the Iran situation in relation to your investments? Have you made any changes since February?</p>
<p>Do you have significant Canadian energy exposure, and have you thought about what happens if oil prices fall 20-25% on a diplomatic breakthrough?</p>
<p>Are you aware that the CUSMA trade agreement comes up for review in July and that Canadian exporters could be significantly affected depending on the outcome?</p>
<p>Is there someone helping you think through these scenarios systematically, or are you trying to manage it in real time?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
