<div style="font-family:''Courier New'',monospace;font-size:10px;color:#999999;padding:4px 14px 10px;font-style:italic;">Source: CRA; WealthNorth Capital Gains Inclusion Rate 2026; CBC News January 2026.</div>
</div>
</div>

<h2>TFSA as the First Line of Defence</h2>
<p>The TFSA remains the most powerful and underutilized planning tool for clients with meaningful non-registered capital gains exposure. Capital gains inside a TFSA are completely tax-free. With cumulative room now at $102,000 for eligible Canadians, the strategic question for any client with significant embedded gains in non-registered accounts is straightforward: are they maximizing TFSA room before realizing gains elsewhere?</p>
<p>The $250,000 individual threshold resets each calendar year, creating an annual planning opportunity to realize gains strategically across years rather than triggering a large gain in a single year at the higher rate. Clients with energy holdings that have appreciated substantially since February, or U.S. equity positions that have risen sharply since late March, may find that 2026 is the right year to realize some gains and establish a new cost base. That conversation requires a full picture of other capital gains, capital loss carry-forwards, and account structure, and it requires it well before year-end.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with non-registered portfolios are vaguely aware that capital gains tax changed but often do not know precisely how it affects them. Incorporated clients are anxious about their corporate accounts and suspect the change is significant but have not yet had a detailed conversation. Many are waiting for the advisor to bring this up.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">The capital gains inclusion rate change that took effect January 1 is now real and in effect, and given what markets have done since February, there are specific clients whose portfolios need a review in light of it. The short version: for individuals, gains above $250,000 in a year are now taxed at an inclusion rate of two-thirds rather than one-half. For corporations, it''s two-thirds on everything. The oil rally and the S&P 500 reaching record highs have created embedded gains in a lot of portfolios that didn''t look like a problem in November but look different today. We need to look at whether your non-registered account has gains above that $250,000 threshold, what your TFSA room looks like, and whether your asset location is optimized given the new rate. This is exactly the kind of review the current environment demands.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> Incorporated professionals and small business owners with corporate investment accounts, where all capital gains now face the two-thirds rate; individual clients with significant non-registered energy or U.S. equity holdings approaching or exceeding the $250,000 annual threshold.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> Clients with non-registered diversified equity portfolios where unrealized gains are growing but gain magnitude is below the threshold.</p>
<p><strong>Minimal near-term impact:</strong> Clients whose assets are primarily inside TFSAs, RRSPs, or other registered accounts; clients whose annual capital gains are consistently below $250,000 and who are not incorporated.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull non-registered account statements for clients with energy sector or U.S. equity holdings and calculate estimated unrealized gains at current prices.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Identify incorporated clients and schedule a corporate investment account review for the impact of the flat 66.67% inclusion rate.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Verify each relevant client''s remaining TFSA contribution room and document in the client file.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Review client portfolios for optimal asset location: are high-gain-potential assets inside registered accounts where possible?</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag clients with capital loss carry-forwards who may be positioned to realize gains tax-efficiently in 2026.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Capital gains tax update and your 2026 review<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
The new capital gains inclusion rate structure that took effect January 1, 2026 is now relevant to your portfolio in a specific way. For individual Canadians, gains above $250,000 in a calendar year are included in income at two-thirds rather than one-half. For corporate investment accounts, the two-thirds rate applies to all capital gains with no threshold.<br><br>
Given the significant gains in energy stocks since February and U.S. equities now at all-time highs, I want to schedule a review to look at your non-registered holdings, your TFSA room, and your asset location strategy. The planning decisions made now can make a meaningful difference to your after-tax position for the full year. I''ll be in touch to set up time.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>Incorporated professionals without an advisor:</strong> Doctors, lawyers, dentists, and other professionals with corporate investment accounts now face a higher tax rate on all capital gains and most are not actively managing for it.</p>
<p><strong>Clients considering large dispositions in 2026:</strong> Business sales, investment property dispositions, or large equity positions approaching the $250,000 threshold are exactly the situations where professional guidance has measurable, calculable dollar value.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I''m reaching out because the capital gains inclusion rate that changed January 1 has specific implications for incorporated professionals, and given what markets have done since February, I wanted to check whether you''ve had a chance to look at how it affects your corporate investment account.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>The combination of the new 66.67% inclusion rate on corporate capital gains, a TSX energy rally, U.S. equities at record highs, and a potentially volatile summer creates a window where structured tax planning has a concrete, measurable dollar value. A client who realizes gains optimally across years, maximizes TFSA room, and reviews corporate account asset location will pay meaningfully less tax over time than one who does not.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>Do you hold investments in a corporate account or holding company, and have you reviewed how the January 1 capital gains change affects those holdings?</p>
<p>Do you know what your remaining TFSA contribution room is, and are you currently using it to hold your highest-gain-potential assets?</p>
<p>Have you realized any capital gains so far in 2026, and do you have any capital loss carry-forwards from prior years?</p>
<p>Is anyone currently helping you think about asset location across your registered and non-registered accounts in light of the new tax structure?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Corporate investment accounts and the 2026 capital gains change<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
As of January 1, 2026, all capital gains inside corporate investment accounts are taxed at a two-thirds inclusion rate, up from one-half. For incorporated professionals with significant investment assets inside a corporation, this is a meaningful change that warrants a review.<br><br>
With energy stocks up sharply since February and U.S. equities at all-time highs, many corporate accounts have unrealized gains that are larger than they were six months ago. A structured review of your asset location, TFSA room, and corporate account strategy could make a significant difference to your after-tax position for 2026 and beyond. I''d welcome a brief conversation.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>',
